WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.645
Fake audio detectors work well on the data they
were trained on,

00:00:04.725 --> 00:00:09.199
but their accuracy collapses when they meet audio
from a new dataset.

00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:13.094
Fine-tuning on the new data then makes them forget
the old,

00:00:13.174 --> 00:00:15.801
a problem called catastrophic forgetting.

00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:21.214
This ICML 2023 paper introduces Regularized
Adaptive Weight Modification, or RAWM,

00:00:21.294 --> 00:00:27.155
a continual learning method that adapts how it
modifies network weights based on the ratio

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:29.135
of genuine to fake utterances,

00:00:29.215 --> 00:00:35.670
and adds a regularization term so the model
remembers the feature distribution of earlier datasets.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.980
Without replaying any past samples,

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:43.921
RAWM cuts forgetting to roughly one tenth of naive
fine-tuning and even generalizes beyond

00:00:44.001 --> 00:00:47.750
audio to speech emotion recognition and image recognition.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:55.165
Fake audio detection has become critical as speech
synthesis and voice conversion produce human-like speech.

00:00:55.245 --> 00:00:58.064
Detectors perform well on their own dataset,

00:00:58.144 --> 00:01:03.071
but their equal error rate rises dramatically on
audio from another dataset.

00:01:03.151 --> 00:01:06.035
The obvious fix, fine-tuning on the new data,

00:01:06.115 --> 00:01:09.461
causes the network to forget what it learned before.

00:01:09.541 --> 00:01:12.557
Earlier remedies require replaying old samples,

00:01:12.637 --> 00:01:16.510
which is impractical when the original data is
inaccessible.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.450
Existing weight-modification methods like OWM treat
every input the same when constraining updates.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:24.090
But in fake audio detection,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.730
genuine speech tends to look similar from one
dataset to the next,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.370
while the fake speech varies.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.010
That regularity is an opportunity: the direction of
a weight update should adapt to how

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.650
much of a batch is genuine versus fake.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:42.290
At the same time,

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:45.930
some datasets collect genuine audio under acoustic
conditions so different that a naive rule backfires,

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.570
motivating an extra safeguard.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.513
The authors contribute Regularized Adaptive Weight
Modification.

00:01:53.593 --> 00:01:57.156
It has two essential steps.

00:01:57.236 --> 00:02:00.799
First,

00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:04.442
adaptive weight modification introduces an extra
projector that adjusts the update direction

00:02:04.522 --> 00:02:08.085
according to the ratio of classes with similar
feature distribution,

00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:11.728
such as genuine utterances, to the others.

00:02:11.808 --> 00:02:15.371
Second, a regularization term, inspired by learning
without forgetting,

00:02:15.451 --> 00:02:19.014
keeps the new inference distribution close to the old one.

00:02:19.094 --> 00:02:22.657
The method needs no previous samples,

00:02:22.737 --> 00:02:26.300
and the authors show it transfers to speech emotion
recognition and image recognition.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.370
RAWM starts from the orthogonal projector P of OWM,

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.140
which points the update away from the subspace
spanned by previous inputs.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.910
On top of it,

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:41.680
the method builds a second projector Q that is
orthogonal to P and scaled by the ratio

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.450
beta of genuine to fake utterances in the batch.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:49.220
The two projectors are normalized and combined into
a modified direction R,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.990
so when a batch is mostly genuine the update leans
toward preserving old knowledge,

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.760
and otherwise it leans toward learning the new data.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.530
For datasets recorded under very different conditions,

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.300
a regularization term treats the frozen pre-trained
model as a teacher and forces the

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.070
fine-tuned student to match its softened outputs,

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.840
remembering the old inference distribution.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.610
Crucially, none of this replays past samples.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:20.227
Experiments run on four fake audio datasets in a
continual-learning sequence: ASVspoof 2019 LA as the source,

00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:24.545
then ASVspoof 2015, the Voice Conversion Challenge
2020 set,

00:03:24.625 --> 00:03:28.862
and the In-the-Wild dataset.

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:33.180
Each is a distinct acoustic and linguistic condition,

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.497
with In-the-Wild being real-world deepfakes of
public figures.

00:03:37.577 --> 00:03:41.815
Detection quality is reported as Equal Error Rate.

00:03:41.895 --> 00:03:46.132
To show breadth,

00:03:46.212 --> 00:03:50.450
the method is also evaluated on speech emotion
recognition and on the CLEAR-10 image recognition benchmark.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:58.277
The headline finding is that RAWM cuts catastrophic
forgetting to roughly one tenth of naive fine-tuning,

00:03:58.357 --> 00:04:01.718
while also halving the error on the new dataset.

00:04:01.798 --> 00:04:05.015
Across two-dataset and four-dataset sequences,

00:04:05.095 --> 00:04:11.682
it achieves the lowest equal error rate on both old
and new datasets compared with mainstream

00:04:11.762 --> 00:04:14.621
continual learning methods including EWC,

00:04:14.701 --> 00:04:17.704
LwF, OWM, and the fake-audio-specific DFWF.

00:04:17.784 --> 00:04:21.431
With the regularization coefficient set to one half,

00:04:21.511 --> 00:04:24.514
giving equal attention to old and new data,

00:04:24.594 --> 00:04:29.890
RAWM keeps error low across all four datasets even
as a baseline collapses.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.831
An ablation separates the two components.

00:04:32.911 --> 00:04:36.889
When old and new datasets share a similar feature
distribution,

00:04:36.969 --> 00:04:40.175
adaptive weight modification does most of the work,

00:04:40.255 --> 00:04:42.558
and removing it sharply raises error.

00:04:42.638 --> 00:04:46.617
When the datasets are recorded under very different
conditions,

00:04:46.697 --> 00:04:50.804
the regularization term becomes the key to
overcoming forgetting.

00:04:50.884 --> 00:04:53.252
Across the full four-dataset sequence,

00:04:53.332 --> 00:04:58.276
removing adaptive weight modification hurts more
than removing regularization,

00:04:58.356 --> 00:05:02.850
so it is the primary driver, with regularization a
valuable complement.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:07.351
In numbers: forgetting drops to about one tenth of
fine-tuning,

00:05:07.431 --> 00:05:10.018
and new-dataset error to about one half.

00:05:10.098 --> 00:05:13.952
In the few-sample regime with only one hundred new
samples,

00:05:14.032 --> 00:05:21.020
RAWM scores an equal error rate of zero point nine
two on the old set and zero point three one on the new,

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.554
far ahead of fine-tuning's near eight.

00:05:23.634 --> 00:05:29.422
On speech emotion recognition it reaches about
forty-two percent accuracy on MSP-Podcast

00:05:29.502 --> 00:05:31.689
and fifty-four percent on IEMOCAP,

00:05:31.769 --> 00:05:35.023
the best of all continual learning methods tested.

00:05:35.103 --> 00:05:38.090
The optimal regularization weight is one half.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:45.031
The takeaway is simple: you can teach a fake audio
detector new datasets without it forgetting the old,

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:48.062
and without keeping any of the old data around.

00:05:48.142 --> 00:05:53.671
RAWM does this by making the weight update adapt to
how genuine-heavy each batch is and

00:05:53.751 --> 00:05:57.540
by regularizing the model to remember its previous
behavior.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.698
Because the underlying regularity, some classes
staying similar across datasets,

00:06:02.778 --> 00:06:04.310
appears in many problems,

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:09.210
the same recipe extends to speech emotion
recognition and image recognition.
